before.en:
\begin{abstract}
Science maps use relationships between papers to describe how research is organised. When these relationships come from text embeddings, a model turns each paper into a list of numbers. Would another model give the same picture? I compare ten models using 500,000 OpenAlex publications from 2000--2024 across 26 disciplines, including Medicine and Physics. Representing disciplines by their papers' average positions produces similar arrangements across models. This holds when other papers are selected to calculate those averages. Yet within a discipline and period, two models share, on average, only eight of the 25 papers closest to a given paper. Giving a model each paper's title alone, instead of its title and abstract, also changes much of that list. The effect varies between models. One model can place a discipline's papers closer together than another's, while another model reverses that order. This comparison uses angles between paper positions. Moving their reference point can change the answer even when selecting other papers does not. These checks show which relationships are shared across representations and which depend on the model, input text or reference point. Agreement about the broad arrangement can coexist with different answers about nearby papers and comparisons between disciplines.
\end{abstract}
\textbf{Keywords:} science mapping; document embeddings; robustness; neighbourhood agreement; geometric comparisons.

